WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.701 --> 00:00:03.467
♪♪

00:00:03.467 --> 00:00:05.601
-Did a show last weekend

00:00:05.601 --> 00:00:08.501
in my hometown --
Manchester, New Hampshire.

00:00:08.501 --> 00:00:10.567
It's where I went
to high school.

00:00:10.567 --> 00:00:16.467
And before the show,
the outgoing mayor, Joyce Craig,

00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:20.901
gave me this --
a key to the city.

00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:24.968
Very cool.

00:00:24.968 --> 00:00:28.901
Think a lot of people would
hang this on the wall

00:00:28.901 --> 00:00:31.300
so they could see it
every day...

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.033
feel proud about themselves.

00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:38.300
But I'm not that kind of guy.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.868
I'm gonna put it on the floor.

00:00:39.868 --> 00:00:42.000
[ Laughter ]

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.834
Because I'm low-key.

00:00:43.834 --> 00:00:45.434
[ Laughter ]

00:00:48.267 --> 00:00:49.734
See me next week.

00:00:49.734 --> 00:00:50.734
[ Laughter ]

00:00:50.734 --> 00:00:53.501
No?

00:00:53.501 --> 00:00:54.601
Alright.

00:00:56.801 --> 00:00:59.067
It's gonna be hard to follow,
though.

00:01:03.934 --> 00:01:06.934
I said Harry Potter
wouldn't wear New Balance.

00:01:06.934 --> 00:01:10.400
And it turns out
there's a brand-new shoe

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.501
made specifically for wizards.

00:01:15.767 --> 00:01:18.667
HOKAS POKAS.

00:01:18.734 --> 00:01:22.501
HOKAS POKAS.

00:01:24.968 --> 00:01:26.200
See me next week.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.601
[ Laughter ]

00:01:27.601 --> 00:01:29.234
No?

00:01:29.234 --> 00:01:30.667
[ Laughter ]

00:01:35.334 --> 00:01:37.467
Um...

00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:40.400
"Harry Potter" took place
in 1991.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.100
Came out in 1997.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.367
I remember when it came out,

00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:48.267
a lot of my friends
read "Harry Potter."

00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:49.467
Former friends.

00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:52.434
I could not stay friends

00:01:52.434 --> 00:01:54.868
with people in their 20s
who was reading --

00:01:54.868 --> 00:01:59.534
who were reading books
about, you know, boy wizards.

00:01:59.534 --> 00:02:01.934
I was busy reading another book

00:02:01.934 --> 00:02:05.534
that came out
in the late '90s.

00:02:05.534 --> 00:02:07.334
"Infinite Jest."

00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:13.400
And I'm almost done with it.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.901
[ Laughter ]

00:02:16.501 --> 00:02:19.267
All tortoises are turtles.
Good for them.

00:02:19.267 --> 00:02:21.133
[ Laughter ]

00:02:26.901 --> 00:02:29.767
I said, "Begs the question."

00:02:29.767 --> 00:02:33.534
Many of you said
that was incorrect.

00:02:33.534 --> 00:02:34.868
Based on the sentence structure,

00:02:34.868 --> 00:02:37.701
I should have said,
"Raises the question."

00:02:37.701 --> 00:02:43.467
So I looked it up.
And here's what I found.

00:02:43.467 --> 00:02:45.901
"The fallacy
of begging the question

00:02:45.901 --> 00:02:48.934
occurs when
an argument's premise

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:51.300
assumes the truth
of the conclusion

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:52.934
instead of supporting it.

00:02:52.934 --> 00:02:56.667
In other words, you assume
without proof the stand position

00:02:56.667 --> 00:02:59.467
or a significant part of
the stand that is in question.

00:02:59.467 --> 00:03:03.734
Begging the question is also
called arguing in a circle."

00:03:03.734 --> 00:03:07.767
So, thanks to that definition
being super-clear...

00:03:07.767 --> 00:03:10.300
[ Laughter ]

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:13.067
...I wrote up an example.

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:14.767
A jackal said "Corrections"

00:03:14.767 --> 00:03:16.868
was boring, self-indulgent,
and long,

00:03:16.868 --> 00:03:19.067
begging the question,
[bleep] do I care?

00:03:19.067 --> 00:03:20.400
[ Laughter ]

00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:28.334
We, in "A Closer Look,"
had Trump --

00:03:28.334 --> 00:03:32.767
transcripts of Trump going
back and forth with the judge.

00:03:32.767 --> 00:03:35.534
Trump saying, "Am not."
Judge saying --

00:03:35.534 --> 00:03:38.767
Sorry. Trump said, "Am too."
Judge said, "Am not."

00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:40.067
"Am too." "Am not."

00:03:40.067 --> 00:03:44.267
People pointed out --
Trump should have said, "Am to,"

00:03:44.267 --> 00:03:47.067
the judge should have said,
"Are not."

00:03:47.067 --> 00:03:49.734
That is correct.
We made a mistake.

00:03:49.734 --> 00:03:51.200
I went to Sal and I said,

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:55.868
"I don't think you're
paying enough attention

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:59.133
to the granular details
of the 'Closer Look' scripts."

00:03:59.133 --> 00:04:01.367
And he said, "Am too."

00:04:01.367 --> 00:04:03.067
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.534 --> 00:04:07.701
We did a bit where Sidney Powell

00:04:07.701 --> 00:04:10.901
said the election was stolen

00:04:10.901 --> 00:04:15.834
by the ghosts of Chester Cheetah
and Mr. Peanut.

00:04:15.834 --> 00:04:17.567
This upset a lot of you.

00:04:17.567 --> 00:04:19.100
[ Laughter ]

00:04:20.934 --> 00:04:23.701
Because you all pointed out,
"You should have said,

00:04:23.701 --> 00:04:26.400
'Chester Cheetah
and the ghost of Mr. Peanut.'"

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.667
Because Mr. Peanut did die.

00:04:29.667 --> 00:04:32.267
In a --
He died in January of 2020.

00:04:32.267 --> 00:04:35.601
This is -- This feels
like a bit. This is not a bit.

00:04:35.601 --> 00:04:37.067
He died in an ad campaign.

00:04:37.067 --> 00:04:39.000
It was part of
a Super Bowl ad campaign.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.801
Planters thought they could
get away with killing Mr. Peanut

00:04:41.801 --> 00:04:44.033
because it's never sad

00:04:44.033 --> 00:04:46.567
when someone who wears a monocle
dies.

00:04:46.567 --> 00:04:48.400
[ Laughter ]

00:04:53.601 --> 00:04:56.467
We said Rudy Giuliani did
a paint-by-numbers of a unicorn,

00:04:56.467 --> 00:04:58.133
and then the graphic
was very clearly

00:04:58.133 --> 00:05:01.767
a connect-the-dots of a unicorn.

00:05:01.767 --> 00:05:03.601
So, of course,
I marched down to Graphics

00:05:03.601 --> 00:05:08.033
and I said, um...
"I'm very angry."

00:05:08.033 --> 00:05:11.033
And they were like --
[Groaning]

00:05:11.033 --> 00:05:12.801
"We hope you're not mad at us."

00:05:12.801 --> 00:05:14.133
And I'm like,
"I am mad at you."

00:05:14.133 --> 00:05:16.667
They're like,
"Ohh. What did we do?"

00:05:16.667 --> 00:05:18.567
[ Laughter ]

00:05:18.567 --> 00:05:21.067
And I was like, "Well, you know,
what bothers me is you --

00:05:21.067 --> 00:05:23.601
I asked for paint-by-numbers,
and you did connect-the-dots.

00:05:23.601 --> 00:05:25.234
And I think you did that
on purpose

00:05:25.234 --> 00:05:27.968
because I think your kink
is me coming down here

00:05:27.968 --> 00:05:29.434
and being upset,"
and they were like,

00:05:29.434 --> 00:05:32.200
"Ohh! Look who's
connecting the dots now!"

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:33.968
[ Laughter ]

00:05:36.968 --> 00:05:38.868
UH, I mimed --
Well, I didn't mime.

00:05:38.868 --> 00:05:42.767
We had -- We had fake cigarettes
and I was smoking.

00:05:42.767 --> 00:05:44.667
And I was told
by a bunch of people

00:05:44.667 --> 00:05:46.634
that's not how
you smoke a cigarette.

00:05:46.634 --> 00:05:49.934
And it's true. I don't know
how to smoke. Never smoked.

00:05:49.934 --> 00:05:53.334
And so I can't correctly,
you know, fake-smoke and, uh...

00:05:55.868 --> 00:06:00.767
I'll regret for the rest of
my life that I didn't smoke.

00:06:00.767 --> 00:06:03.901
So I don't know how to do it.

00:06:03.901 --> 00:06:07.734
The rest of my life,
which based on actuarial tables

00:06:07.734 --> 00:06:10.334
will be a great deal longer
than those of you who do.

00:06:10.334 --> 00:06:12.934
[ Laughter ]

00:06:12.934 --> 00:06:14.901
[ Sighs ]

00:06:18.501 --> 00:06:22.000
Mr. Pibb...

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:27.367
was rebranded to Pibb Xtra
in 2001.

00:06:28.667 --> 00:06:32.467
I guess what happened
was Mr. Pibb got a divorce.

00:06:32.467 --> 00:06:33.968
Then, like a lot
of divorced guys,

00:06:33.968 --> 00:06:37.300
he wanted to let everybody know
he was, like, fun now.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:38.634
So he changed it up.

00:06:38.634 --> 00:06:41.467
Very funny
that they didn't realize

00:06:41.467 --> 00:06:45.934
the problem with the name
Mr. Pibb was "Pibb."

00:06:45.934 --> 00:06:48.267
Like, to change the name
and keep the "Pibb,"

00:06:48.267 --> 00:06:49.567
which is just a --

00:06:49.567 --> 00:06:53.667
It's just unsatisfying
to say "Pibb."

00:06:53.667 --> 00:06:55.634
But, yeah.
Don't worry about Mrs. Pibb.

00:06:55.634 --> 00:06:57.701
She, after the divorce,
married Mr. Peanut.

00:06:57.701 --> 00:07:00.734
Then he died,
and she's rolling in it.

00:07:00.734 --> 00:07:03.501
Cha-ching!

00:07:03.501 --> 00:07:05.767
Cha-ching! She's rolling.

00:07:05.767 --> 00:07:07.801
You know what she's rolling in?
Legume money.

00:07:07.801 --> 00:07:09.634
[ Laughter ]

00:07:11.167 --> 00:07:14.801
"They call me Mr. Pibb!"

00:07:14.801 --> 00:07:16.267
[ Laughter ]

00:07:18.934 --> 00:07:20.634
I knew I'd get it in.

00:07:20.634 --> 00:07:23.601
[ Laughter ]

00:07:23.601 --> 00:07:26.434
Oh. This one.

00:07:26.434 --> 00:07:27.834
Again, the smoking thing.

00:07:27.834 --> 00:07:29.901
I was like,
"Hey, we're gonna do it.

00:07:29.901 --> 00:07:33.133
And this brings us to new bit,
"Seth Mutters for a Second."

00:07:33.133 --> 00:07:36.767
"Stares Off into the Distance
and Mutters for a Second."

00:07:36.767 --> 00:07:40.534
A bunch of you said
that was 47 seconds.

00:07:40.534 --> 00:07:41.968
That was more than a second.

00:07:41.968 --> 00:07:43.534
And my kids do that
all the time, too.

00:07:43.534 --> 00:07:45.234
Like, I'll say,
"Can you give me a second?"

00:07:45.234 --> 00:07:46.868
And they'll be like,
"That was a second!"

00:07:46.868 --> 00:07:48.300
And let me just say
to all of you,

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:50.634
my kids included --
funny every time you do it.

00:07:50.634 --> 00:07:52.534
[ Laughter ]

00:07:58.133 --> 00:08:01.267
Uh, an expert reached out.

00:08:01.267 --> 00:08:03.334
And you know
what kind of experts

00:08:03.334 --> 00:08:06.200
leave comments in the
"Corrections" YouTube page?

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:08.400
The kind whose credentials
you can't check.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:10.701
[ Laughter ]

00:08:10.701 --> 00:08:13.000
Expert reached out and said,
"Most animal venoms

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:16.267
would not be poisonous
to ingest."

00:08:16.267 --> 00:08:17.968
So bottoms up.

00:08:17.968 --> 00:08:19.434
[ Laughter ]

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:23.901
Envenomation.
I learned this word.

00:08:23.901 --> 00:08:28.834
Envenomation is exposure
to a toxin or poison

00:08:28.834 --> 00:08:32.934
resulting from a bite or sting
from an animal.

00:08:32.934 --> 00:08:35.968
Devenomation is when you get
bit by a poisonous animal

00:08:35.968 --> 00:08:37.400
and fall out a window.

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:39.234
[ Telephone ringing ]

00:08:41.567 --> 00:08:44.734
Hello? Mm-hmm.

00:08:44.734 --> 00:08:45.934
Okay.

00:08:48.667 --> 00:08:50.801
I was just told
that was an old callback.

00:08:50.801 --> 00:08:52.567
[ Laughter ]

00:09:09.067 --> 00:09:11.300
What are we doing?!

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:14.234
What is this?!

00:09:14.234 --> 00:09:18.234
Why do I spend all day,
like, e-mailing people,

00:09:18.234 --> 00:09:20.167
asking them for an old phone?!

00:09:20.167 --> 00:09:22.367
[ Laughter ]

00:09:22.367 --> 00:09:24.767
Like, what do I think
is gonna happen?!

00:09:24.767 --> 00:09:27.534
[ Laughter ]

00:09:27.534 --> 00:09:29.067
[ Sighs ]

00:09:36.033 --> 00:09:38.534
Last week in "Corrections."
I called it Shoemaker's wedding,

00:09:38.534 --> 00:09:40.367
and a lot of you said
it wasn't Shoemaker's wedding.

00:09:40.367 --> 00:09:41.801
It was Shoemaker's daughter's
wedding.

00:09:41.801 --> 00:09:43.200
But I can tell you,
around the office,

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:44.934
when you call it Shoemaker's
daughter's wedding,

00:09:44.934 --> 00:09:47.434
he will say,
"Oh, she paid for it?!"

00:09:47.434 --> 00:09:48.901
[ Laughter ]

00:09:59.734 --> 00:10:01.601
A lot of you were mad
that we showed

00:10:01.601 --> 00:10:03.868
me stumbling on the end
of "A Closer Look"

00:10:03.868 --> 00:10:06.634
and still didn't tell you
what I was saying

00:10:06.634 --> 00:10:09.367
when it sounded like
I was speaking Welsh.

00:10:09.367 --> 00:10:10.801
What I was saying is,

00:10:10.801 --> 00:10:14.501
"Get angry at prosecutors."

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:18.400
Let's hear and see
how close I was.

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:20.000
Geddankry at prosecutors.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:23.334
[ Laughter ]

00:10:23.334 --> 00:10:27.267
It's like I recorded it
backwards.

00:10:27.267 --> 00:10:29.000
It's like David Lynch.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:30.868
"Geddankry ahproskoter."

00:10:30.868 --> 00:10:32.434
[ Laughter ]

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:38.801
I talked about Jim Breyer
last week,

00:10:38.801 --> 00:10:41.767
and someone wrote,
"Who the hell is Jim Breyer?"

00:10:41.767 --> 00:10:44.133
[ Laughter ]

00:10:44.133 --> 00:10:45.934
I'm not --
I'm not gonna tell you,

00:10:45.934 --> 00:10:48.234
but I will tell you
those would be the...

00:10:48.234 --> 00:10:50.400
last words you ever said...

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:53.934
[ Laughter ]

00:10:53.934 --> 00:10:56.734
...if he was within earshot.

00:10:56.734 --> 00:10:59.067
"Hey, uh, Jim Breyer says
you can't stay in there."

00:10:59.067 --> 00:11:01.334
"Who the hell Is Jim Breyer?!

00:11:01.334 --> 00:11:02.534
Ohh!"

00:11:02.534 --> 00:11:04.834
[ Laughter ]

00:11:09.167 --> 00:11:14.167
People who go to Ren Faires
are called Rennies.

00:11:14.167 --> 00:11:16.601
So keep that in mind
if you're ever yelling at one

00:11:16.601 --> 00:11:18.434
out an open car window.

00:11:18.434 --> 00:11:20.234
[ Laughter ]

00:11:22.267 --> 00:11:25.267
Carnival workers are called
carnival workers.

00:11:27.133 --> 00:11:28.400
[ Laughter ]

00:11:32.801 --> 00:11:34.234
Hey. We had a thing.

00:11:34.234 --> 00:11:37.200
We were talking about how
the new Speaker of the House

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:41.067
and his son were on an app,

00:11:41.067 --> 00:11:43.601
told each other when
they were watching pornography.

00:11:43.601 --> 00:11:46.300
It's like a pornography
monitoring app.

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:49.400
And then we said, "Thank God
Joe Biden and Hunter

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:51.968
don't have that
on each other's phone."

00:11:51.968 --> 00:11:54.901
And then, um...

00:11:54.901 --> 00:11:58.601
I had a -- Joe -- His phone was
bing, bing, bing, bing bing.

00:11:58.601 --> 00:12:01.267
And he took it, and he --
One of the things
I had him say was,

00:12:01.267 --> 00:12:04.367
"Ah, come on, Hunter.
Get ahold of yourself."

00:12:04.367 --> 00:12:06.501
More than one of you said if
Hunter was watching pornography,

00:12:06.501 --> 00:12:08.734
he would likely already
have ahold of himself.

00:12:08.734 --> 00:12:10.501
[ Laughter ]

00:12:24.567 --> 00:12:27.767
There just wasn't a lot
this week.

00:12:27.767 --> 00:12:30.400
I know we have to fill it
for the advertisers.

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:33.267
[ Laughter ]

00:12:33.267 --> 00:12:35.200
At a certain length.

00:12:37.601 --> 00:12:39.400
Just won't run their ads.

00:12:44.801 --> 00:12:48.133
We talked about
"Silence of the Lambs."

00:12:48.133 --> 00:12:50.434
Was it a well?

00:12:50.434 --> 00:12:52.200
Was it a cistern?

00:12:53.834 --> 00:12:57.667
There's a French word for it
I always forget.

00:12:57.667 --> 00:12:59.934
Oh. Oubliette.

00:12:59.934 --> 00:13:01.334
[ Woman laughs ]

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:07.000
All I wanted was one. Thank you.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:08.400
[ Laughter ]

00:13:10.534 --> 00:13:12.300
The most heartbreaking thing
that happened

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:15.234
is you covered your mouth.

00:13:15.234 --> 00:13:17.734
If she does that again,
slap those [bleep] hands away!

00:13:17.734 --> 00:13:19.567
[ Laughter ]

00:13:26.367 --> 00:13:29.868
So, someone said, "Is it a well
or was it a cistern?"

00:13:29.868 --> 00:13:34.434
So a very actual helpful
Jackal...

00:13:34.434 --> 00:13:36.200
went to the book,

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:38.400
the Thomas Harris book
"Silence of the Lambs,"

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:40.100
because there
there is clarity.

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:41.934
These are the words
describing it.

00:13:41.934 --> 00:13:43.434
"In the basement room

00:13:43.434 --> 00:13:47.133
directly beneath the kitchen
was a well, long dry.

00:13:47.133 --> 00:13:52.000
Its stone rim reinforced
with modern well rings."

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:54.067
He wanted us to know
it was a well so much,

00:13:54.067 --> 00:13:57.133
he put in
that "well rings" part.

00:13:57.133 --> 00:13:59.601
Really hammered it home.

00:13:59.601 --> 00:14:02.033
"The floor, a well floor."

00:14:02.033 --> 00:14:04.801
[ Laughter ]

00:14:04.801 --> 00:14:06.267
"Of that one could be certain.

00:14:06.267 --> 00:14:07.968
The doorbell rang.

00:14:07.968 --> 00:14:10.400
Jame Gumb opened
to reveal Agent Starling.

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:11.701
'How are you doing?' she asked.

00:14:11.701 --> 00:14:14.434
'Good,' he said, knowing
it was incorrect grammatically

00:14:14.434 --> 00:14:17.868
but desperate not to
get her thinking about wells."

00:14:17.868 --> 00:14:19.501
[ Laughter ]

00:14:25.267 --> 00:14:26.968
You know what
this "Corrections" is?

00:14:26.968 --> 00:14:28.400
Not for everybody.

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:29.601
[ Laughter ]

00:14:31.501 --> 00:14:33.734
Um, on Halloween -- I forgot
to show this last week.

00:14:33.734 --> 00:14:38.033
On Halloween, an audience member
came in costume,

00:14:38.033 --> 00:14:40.767
and her costume
was Cue Card Wally.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:46.400
And I will just say,
while we'll allow it once,

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:49.534
people dressing like Wally
is not helpful...

00:14:50.767 --> 00:14:53.033
...in regards to what we're
affectionately referring to

00:14:53.033 --> 00:14:54.501
as our Wally Problem.

00:14:54.501 --> 00:14:56.100
[ Laughter ]

00:15:04.434 --> 00:15:07.400
Oh, you know, when we have
a music guest,

00:15:07.400 --> 00:15:10.968
I'll hold up the album art.

00:15:10.968 --> 00:15:12.667
Because someone
referred to the fact

00:15:12.667 --> 00:15:14.834
that I was holding
the vinyl wrong.

00:15:14.834 --> 00:15:17.567
It is not a vinyl.
I am not holding an album.

00:15:17.567 --> 00:15:19.234
It is simply the album art

00:15:19.234 --> 00:15:22.367
cut to be the size of an album.

00:15:22.367 --> 00:15:26.400
And I -- I did not know that
when I started the show.

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:28.300
I had -- Obviously like
the rest of you,

00:15:28.300 --> 00:15:30.534
I'd watch shows and I had
assumed it was an album.

00:15:30.534 --> 00:15:32.434
And I was so excited
when I got this show

00:15:32.434 --> 00:15:35.701
that I would get an album

00:15:35.701 --> 00:15:38.167
every time we had
a musical guest.

00:15:38.167 --> 00:15:39.601
And just imagine now.

00:15:39.601 --> 00:15:42.200
Imagine, after all the shows
we've done,

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:45.801
if I had an album from
every musical guest we'd had.

00:15:45.801 --> 00:15:47.501
Just imagine --
If that was true,

00:15:47.501 --> 00:15:50.701
imagine how many times my wife,
Alexi, would have said to me,

00:15:50.701 --> 00:15:52.701
"So do you have, like, a plan?"

00:15:52.701 --> 00:15:55.100
[ Laughter ]

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:56.767
"For all these records?"

00:15:56.767 --> 00:15:58.501
[ Laughter ]

00:16:05.367 --> 00:16:07.334
This is what I forgot last week.

00:16:10.901 --> 00:16:12.801
Got another one of these
in the mail.

00:16:15.934 --> 00:16:20.868
♪ Nothing in the world
could be worse than me ♪

00:16:20.868 --> 00:16:25.400
♪ I'm the worst thing
that you'll ever see ♪

00:16:25.400 --> 00:16:27.968
♪ Excuse me real quick,
what did you say? ♪

00:16:27.968 --> 00:16:30.033
[ Laughter ]

00:16:30.033 --> 00:16:33.734
♪ I'm worse than you
each and every day ♪

00:16:33.734 --> 00:16:36.467
♪ We're gonna [bleep]
in your nightmares ♪

00:16:36.467 --> 00:16:38.567
[ Laughter ]

00:16:47.133 --> 00:16:48.834
Okay.

00:16:48.834 --> 00:16:50.133
[ Laughter ]

00:16:53.634 --> 00:16:55.501
Can we -- Can we say
another thing?

00:16:55.501 --> 00:16:57.934
It -- They're -- By the way...

00:16:57.934 --> 00:17:00.067
those are tails.

00:17:00.067 --> 00:17:02.334
[ Laughter ]

00:17:04.567 --> 00:17:06.267
...like this.

00:17:06.267 --> 00:17:08.467
[ Laughter ]

00:17:15.834 --> 00:17:17.400
[ Mouthing words ]

00:17:17.400 --> 00:17:18.734
[ Sighs ]

00:17:18.734 --> 00:17:20.267
[ Laughter ]

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:34.801
See me next week.

